66:/// The largest number of public inputs a single contract call may declare.
67:///
68:/// A call's public inputs are two fixed elements (the binding input and the
69:/// communication commitment) followed by the field representation of its
70:/// guaranteed and fallible transcripts' programs; see
71:/// [`Transcript::public_inputs_len`].
72:///
73:/// This is a backstop against `Op::Noop { n }`, the one opcode whose
74:/// public-input footprint is not proportional to its own encoding: `n` is a
75:/// `u32` carried in a 9-byte op, and each unit of it contributes a 32-byte
76:/// field element, so an uncapped `n` turns a 77-byte contract call into a
77:/// 128 GiB allocation. Every other opcode contributes a number of elements
78:/// bounded by its own size, and hence by the transaction byte limit.
79:///
80:/// The value is anchored on the verifier parameters: `PARAMS_VERIFIER` is a
81:/// 2^14 setup, so no circuit the ledger can verify against -- and therefore no
82:/// statement it accepts -- exceeds 2^14 rows. This leaves 4x headroom over
83:/// that, and caps a single call's public inputs at 2 MiB.
84:///
85:/// Anything that materializes a call's public inputs from an untrusted
86:/// transcript should check its count against this first.
